  "answer_markdown": "## The biggest bottleneck: purchase-path integrity first; checkout completion second\n\nBefore interpreting ads or rewriting the page, fix the **potentially broken buying path**. Public storefront research found the flagship kit simultaneously saying **“Out of stock / Sold out”** and **“Only 48 kits left” + delivery in 2–7 days**. If shoppers see a sold-out CTA, no amount of traffic or creative testing will solve sales. This may be a crawl/theme artifact—not proven live—so verify it immediately on a real mobile and desktop purchase journey.\n\nOnce that is clean, your clearest measured funnel leak is **checkout completion**, not merely traffic:\n\n| Shopify funnel, Aug 24–Sep 22 | Rate |\n|---|---:|\n| Sessions → Added to cart | 112 / 4,452 = **2.5%** |\n| Added to cart → Reached checkout | 101 / 112 = **90.2%** |\n| Reached checkout → Completed checkout | 34 / 101 = **33.7%** |\n\nTwo cautions: Shopify reports 39 orders while the funnel shows 34 completed checkouts, and the test-order count is unknown. Treat these as directional funnel signals, not audited customer counts. But the pattern is still useful: people who add the kit are generally willing to start checkout; too many do not finish.\n\n### Fix this first: a 48-hour checkout + storefront audit\nPlace 2–3 genuine test orders (then cancel/refund as appropriate) using Android/mobile and desktop, different PIN codes, prepaid and COD if offered. Record each failure/friction point.\n\n**Check in this order**\n1. **Availability:** The exact ad landing URL, product page, cart, and checkout all show the kit purchasable. Remove manual “X left” text unless inventory is reliably synced. Never show “sold out” and “48 left” together.\n2. **Price clarity:** Confirm the final checkout amount matches the ₹3,299 product-page expectation; disclose any shipping/COD charge before checkout starts.\n3. **Payment:** Test UPI, cards, and COD (if enabled). Review failed-payment reasons and whether payment options appear correctly by PIN code/device.\n4. **Delivery:** Test serviceability and ETA for major target PIN codes. A late surprise here is a likely abandoner.\n5. **Checkout trust:** Make guarantees and support visible immediately before the buy action and in cart/checkout where your platform permits. Ensure the 30-day return and 720-day eligible-failure replacement terms link to plain-English eligibility/exclusions.\n6. **Event hygiene:** Confirm `ViewContent`, `AddToCart`, `InitiateCheckout`, and `Purchase` each fire once and carry the correct value. Your Meta and Shopify funnels do not reconcile perfectly, so do not optimise aggressively on one report until this is checked.\n\n### What to change on the product page after the audit\nYour product is ₹3,299 in a category where parents worry about adhesive strength, fit, installation, and whether a child can defeat the lock. The public page makes strong reassurance claims, but has limited visible proof. Add proof—not more broad claims—above the fold and around Add to Cart.\n\n**Drop-in purchase block copy (use only after each statement is verified):**\n\n> **Childproof the everyday danger zones—without hunting for six separate products.**  \n> One organised kit for corners, sockets, cabinets, doors and fridge/microwave access.  \n> **Need help setting it up?** Get 1-to-1 installation video support after purchase.  \n> **₹3,299** · [show accurate delivery estimate] · [show only verified payment options]\n\n**Proof strip beneath it:**\n> See what’s included • Check compatibility • Watch installation • Read returns & replacement terms\n\nThen add four concrete modules:\n- **“Will it fit my home?”** Show actual photos/video of 5A and 15A Indian sockets, common cabinet styles, fridge/microwave use cases, and state limitations plainly.\n- **“How it installs”** A 30–60 second real installation video. Include surface-prep, cure time if relevant, removal guidance, and when not to use it.\n- **“What parents received”** Collect and publish only genuine buyer reviews/photos, tagged by item (socket guard, corner protector, lock). Do not manufacture social proof.\n- **“What is covered?”** A concise warranty/returns table. Avoid unsubstantiated comparisons such as competitors having “zero quality checks.”\n\n### Paid-media decision for this week\nDo **not** scale spend until the purchase path is verified. Across Meta, ₹62,996 spend generated 31 attributed purchases, or roughly **₹2,032 reported CPA**. On the supplied unit economics, a ₹3,299 sale has roughly ₹2,113 left before ad spend and before tax/packaging, RTO handling/recovery, support, returns and overheads (₹3,299 − ₹650 product − ₹350 shipping − ~₹66 payment fee). That makes the current blended CPA too close to the ceiling to call healthy.\n\nKeep the active Shopify testing campaign as the control only if its live purchase path passes. Its reported ₹1,693 CPA is better than the account blend, but still leaves only about ₹420 before the unknown costs above. Pause/avoid reviving campaigns that attracted cheap clicks but weak purchase intent; the “Low CPM” campaign produced very cheap visits but only 2 purchases from ₹5,065 spend.\n\n### Success criteria before the next creative test\n- No conflicting stock or delivery messages anywhere in the journey.\n- Every payment method and target PIN code completes successfully.\n- Checkout-completion rate improves from the current **33.7%** baseline.\n- CPA is below your true allowable CPA after you fill in packaging, taxes, RTO reverse logistics/recovery, refunds and support costs.\n- New creative tests are judged on purchases/checkout quality, not CTR or cheap landing-page views.\n\nIn short: **first prove buyers can buy; next remove checkout friction; then use evidence-led product-page proof to lift add-to-cart rate.**",
